        "title": "Selection Chooses Objects; Focus Chooses Context",
        "answer": "Selection identifies objects for actions such as editing, moving, deleting, inspecting, or operating on several nodes at once. Focus identifies the working region that should receive semantic priority. Five scattered nodes can be selected while only one branch remains focused. This keeps multi-selection compatible with a singular attention model.",
        "confidence": "high",
        "summary": "Selection answers what is chosen. Focus answers where the user is working.",
        "notes": "Hover, search matches, pinned nodes, and editor state should likewise remain independent dimensions.",
        "memo": "",
        "link": "",
        "visibilityRole": "browser",
        "_placement": {
          "version": 1,
          "state": "auto-placed",
          "source": "create",
          "updatedAt": 1786462966587,
