        "_classBinding": {
          "key": "qa-summary",
          "sourceRef": "github://mikemartinez1974/public/templates/question-driven-explanation-template/classes/nodes/qa-summary.node-class.node",
          "ref": "github://mikemartinez1974/public/templates/question-driven-explanation-template/classes/nodes/qa-summary.node-class.node"
        },
        "title": "Focus Model Contract",
        "synthesis": "Twilite focus should begin as a small, authoritative spatial-attention primitive. Each viewport has zero or one active focus region. Selection and other emphasis states remain independent. Branch expansion can move focus deeper through the graph, while a focus path or history may preserve where the user came from without creating simultaneous active regions. Semantic zoom independently produces a base presentation band. A presentation resolver combines that band with focus relationship and explicit node state, allowing focused content to become more informative than the surrounding graph. Renderers consume the final requested surface rather than inventing focus or zoom policy themselves.",
        "keyPoints": "1. Zero or one authoritative focus region per viewport.\n2. Focus means active working context, not generic selection.\n3. Selection may contain many nodes without creating many focus regions.\n4. Branch expansion can move focus deeper.\n5. A focus path can preserve navigation history while only the deepest/current region is active.\n6. Zoom determines the normal presentation baseline.\n7. Focus may promote nodes above that baseline.\n8. Presentation resolution combines zoom, focus relationship, and explicit state.\n9. Node renderers should not own focus policy.\n10. The launch implementation should establish the primitive before adding elaborate focus UX.",
        "openQuestions": "What exact user actions acquire focus, move it, and clear it?\nDoes clicking a node focus only that node or derive a region from graph topology?\nHow is branch membership determined when graphs contain cross-links or non-tree structures?\nShould focus promotion affect edges and labels as well as node surfaces?\nHow much focus history should be retained initially?\nShould opening an editor imply focus, or remain an independent explicit state?\nWhat is the correct fallback when a focused node class does not expose the requested semantic surface?",
        "notes": "This graph describes the intended architecture, not a frozen implementation contract. Codex should inspect current graph/editor state and existing focus-related code before choosing concrete storage locations or gestures.",
        "memo": "",
        "link": "",
        "visibilityRole": "browser",
        "_placement": {
          "version": 1,
          "state": "auto-placed",
          "source": "create",
          "updatedAt": 1786462966587,
          "reason": "node-batch-create"
        }
      }
    }
